WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.960
Disney's live-action version of "Mulan"

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.500
is not a shot-for-shot remake

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.900
of the animated classic from 1998.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.870
Instead, it's more of a reimagining

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.720
based on the ancient Chinese poem

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.440
"The Ballad of Mulan."

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.980
And while so many things are different,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.640
the heart of the story is still there,

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.490
along with plenty of nods
to the animated original.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:21.180
Here are all the ways
Disney's live-action "Mulan"

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.750
is different from the original.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.005
Oh, and warning, spoilers ahead.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.440
When the new movie starts,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.520
Mulan is a child,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.560
and we watch her grow older on screen.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.393
But in the original,

00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:32.770
we see her at only one age

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.190
for the duration of the film.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.030
Mulan's surname is tweaked from Fa,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.390
the Cantonese pronunciation
of her name, to Hua,

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.230
which makes it truer to the
name of the original ballad.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.930
However, both names have
the same basic meaning.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.458
Later, when Mulan needs to come up

00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:46.883
with a fake name for the army,

00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:48.030
she settles on Hua Jun,

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.190
as opposed to Fa Ping
from the original movie.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.730
Mulan: It's Ping.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:52.563
Li Shang: Ping.

00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:54.880
Narrator: There are also some
changes to Mulan's family

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.070
in the live-action version.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.680
She now has a younger sister.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.800
In the animated version,
she's an only child,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.095
though she does have a dog named

00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:02.100
Little Brother.
Mulan: Little Brother!

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.220
Narrator: While in some
versions of the original legend

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.280
she's an only child,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.900
there are other versions
where she has siblings.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.720
Also missing from the live-action version:

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.540
Mulan's quirky grandmother.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.450
Thanks to the film's longer running time,

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.227
they were able to more fully flesh out

00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:17.480
the bond between Mulan and her father.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.360
In the new version,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.878
Mulan carries her father's sword

00:01:19.878 --> 00:01:22.128
with the words "Loyal," "Brave," "True"

00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:23.210
etched into the blade.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.120
The phrase is repeated
throughout the film,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.050
speaking to the theme of the story.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.175
Mulan: Loyal, brave, and true.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.340
Narrator: But take a look at the sword

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.540
in the animated version;

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.260
you'll notice both sides
of it are completely blank.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.550
Another iconic object

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.790
from the original movie remains unchanged:

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.040
Mulan's hair clip with a flower on it.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.740
In both movies, she leaves it behind

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.390
to signify that she's left home.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.230
In the animated movie,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.830
she leaves it by her parents' bed.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:47.970
In the live-action one,

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.350
she leaves it in her father's wardrobe.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.740
But there were some elements
from the animated movie

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.230
that just would not have
worked as well in live action.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:59.000
Specifically, Mulan's
anthropomorphic animal sidekicks,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.010
like Cri-Kee, the lucky cricket.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.410
Cri-Kee was a crucial part
of the matchmaking scene,

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.640
when the troublemaking bug causes chaos

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.600
after soaking in the cup of tea.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.500
In the reimagining, instead of a cricket,

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.920
a spider pops up on the table.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.600
Mulan tries to cover it with a teapot,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.850
ultimately ending in disaster.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.410
Cri-Kee may not be in the movie,

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.900
but he is there in spirit.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.930
One of Mulan's fellow human
soldiers is named Cricket.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.230
And like Cri-Kee, he's
called a good luck charm.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.140
Grandmother Fa: Yep.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.190
This cricket's a lucky one!

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.250
Narrator: Another fan favorite -

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.277
and a controversial character
- missing from the film:

00:02:29.277 --> 00:02:30.460
Mushu the dragon,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.840
who was voiced by Eddie Murphy.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.030
You might recall that
the wisecracking creature

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.150
served as Mulan's guardian
sent by her ancestors.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.370
While Mushu was popular with US audiences,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.830
Chinese viewers found
him to be trivializing.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.380
So, no Mushu means no scene

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.450
where he shatters the
dragon guardian statue

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.840
that is actually supposed
to accompany Mulan.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.160
Instead, it's Mulan

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.090
who accidentally shatters the statue,

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.460
that of a phoenix.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.600
The phoenix makes sense
as a Mushu stand-in,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.390
and not just because the two
creatures have a similar look.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.702
After she leaves for war,

00:02:58.702 --> 00:03:00.726
Mulan's father prays to the ancestors

00:03:00.726 --> 00:03:02.233
and the phoenix to protect her.

00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:05.370
So while there are no actual
ghosts of ancestors seen here,

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.342
we still know they have Mulan's back.

00:03:07.342 --> 00:03:09.800
And the phoenix follows her
silently throughout her journey.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.698
Other characters from the animated version

00:03:11.698 --> 00:03:14.120
are present here, but with new names.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.280
Instead of Shan Yu,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.380
the leader of the invading
northern army is Böri Khan.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.140
And he has more motivations now

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:21.733
than just wanting to conquer China.

00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:24.640
Khan also wants to avenge
his father's death.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.980
Böri Khan is fighting with
the Rourans, not the Huns,

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.840
as Shan Yu was in the original.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.240
This might be more accurate.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.490
Some historians have said

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.050
the Huns were likely more focused

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.680
on attacking Europe than China

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.020
during Mulan's time.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.820
Alongside Böri Khan is
a completely new villain

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.910
to the universe named Xianniang.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.060
In the animated movie,

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:44.830
Shan Yu does have a falcon,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.430
which also acts as his spy,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.230
but the falcon never takes
the form of a person.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.280
According to the filmmakers,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.520
they wanted to bring in
a new female antagonist

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.350
as a way to balance out the protagonist.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.260
See, both characters are able
to harness the power of chi,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.510
a concept that is never
mentioned in the original.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.090
It's apparent that Mulan can
do this early in the film.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.023
And later on, it allows
her to do everything

00:04:04.023 --> 00:04:06.930
from jump across rooftops to scale walls.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.390
Another one of the animated characters

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:09.910
wasn't necessarily removed,

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.530
but rather divided up.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.513
According to the producer Jason Reed,

00:04:13.513 --> 00:04:16.550
Li Shang, the captain who
notably got down to business,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:19.070
was split into two separate characters.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.300
One is Commander Tung, Mulan's mentor,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.045
and the other is a fellow
soldier named Honghui

00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:25.510
who grows close to Mulan.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:27.244
Reed also said it didn't feel appropriate

00:04:27.244 --> 00:04:29.970
having an older commanding
officer be her love interest.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.400
In an interview with Insider,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.810
animated "Mulan" codirector Tony Bancroft

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.190
said that they actually considered

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.247
making Li Shang Mulan's equal.

00:04:37.247 --> 00:04:39.600
Originally, they had another
character as the sergeant,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.200
who would have turned out to be a spy.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.530
Once that character was cut,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.030
Li Shang got a promotion.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.270
One of the most iconic
parts of the animated Mulan

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.178
is missing from this live-action version:

00:04:48.178 --> 00:04:49.094
the songs.

00:04:49.094 --> 00:04:50.900
Instead of making the film a musical,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.280
memorable lines from the songs

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.260
are woven into the dialogue.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.517
For example, Mulan says,

00:04:55.517 --> 00:04:56.657
"Yes, it is best.

00:04:56.657 --> 00:04:58.200
I will bring honor to us all,"

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.412
calling back to the
song "Honor to Us All."

00:05:00.412 --> 00:05:02.477
And while you won't get
to hear Donny Osmond sing

00:05:02.477 --> 00:05:03.740
"I'll Make a Man Out of You,"

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.527
the sergeant does tell the young warriors,

00:05:05.527 --> 00:05:08.110
"We're going to make men out
of every single one of you."

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:09.943
At one point, Mulan sits at a table

00:05:09.943 --> 00:05:11.800
with some fellow soldiers, who talk about

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.833
their favorite characteristics in a woman.

00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:14.710
Ling holds up a drawing

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:16.150
of a woman he'd been matched with

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.600
that looks similar to those sketches

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:18.870
from the animated scene.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:19.703
Po then says,

00:05:19.703 --> 00:05:20.999
"I don't care what she looks like;

00:05:20.999 --> 00:05:22.310
I care what she cooks like,"

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.800
which is very similar to
a line sung by Chien-Po

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.220
in "A Girl Worth Fighting For."

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.410
And while Mulan never
sings the words herself,

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:29.570
the tune for "Reflection"

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:30.760
can be heard on the score

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.480
during several poignant
moments throughout the film.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.190
While the basics of
the story are the same,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.750
some major plot points
have also been altered.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.670
In the animated movie,
the toughest obstacle

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.150
the commander throws at the soldiers

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.520
is making them retrieve an arrow

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.140
from the top of a high pole

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.440
while holding a pair of weights.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.482
In the live-action movie,

00:05:46.482 --> 00:05:48.360
they also must carry full buckets of water

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.000
to the top of a high mountain.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.470
In the animated training montage,

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:52.900
they do train with buckets,

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.570
but it's large sacks they're
carrying up a mountain.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.403
In both movies, Mulan is injured,

00:05:57.403 --> 00:05:58.550
but in different ways.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:05:59.383
In the new movie,

00:05:59.383 --> 00:06:01.460
she's hit by one of
Xianniang's throwing stars.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.730
But in the animated movie,

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.650
she's slashed by Shan Yu's blade,

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.000
which ultimately leads to her

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.580
being stripped in the medical tent

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.040
and revealed as a woman.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.330
One of the most dramatic
moments in Mulan's story

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.230
is when her secret is revealed.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.300
The live-action version

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.213
has her being the one to make the decision

00:06:16.213 --> 00:06:17.580
to out herself as a woman.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.490
In the middle of battle,

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.080
she lets down her hair
and shows her true self.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.710
It's an especially empowering moment

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.780
that gives the character more agency.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.100
This scene also might explain

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.890
why the live-action version doesn't show

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.580
Mulan chopping her long hair off

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:31.210
earlier with her father's blade.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.040
This battle is brought to an abrupt end

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.546
by a huge avalanche,

00:06:34.546 --> 00:06:36.090
a famous scene from the animated movie

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.540
that director Niki Caro

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.670
decided to keep in the live-action film.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.160
But instead of shooting a cannon,

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.102
in the new movie,

00:06:42.102 --> 00:06:43.650
Mulan tricks Böri Khan's men

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.250
into firing a large
boulder at the mountain,

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:49.060
and nobody is seen rising
from the snow unscathed.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.540
This time around, there's no
victory parade in the city.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.130
And while Shan Yu met an unfortunate

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.522
firework-related demise
in the animated movie,

00:06:55.522 --> 00:06:57.320
Böri Khan tumbles to the ground

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.440
before Mulan finishes him off
with one of his own arrows.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.060
And if you're waiting for fireworks,

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.490
that doesn't happen until
Mulan saves the emperor,

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.190
who, played by actor Jet Li,

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.330
has a much more active role in this film,

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:10.010
venturing out to fight his enemy.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.410
When Mulan returns home in
the live-action version,

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:13.397
her father says,

00:07:13.397 --> 00:07:15.490
"It is my daughter that
means everything to me."

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:16.770
In the animated version,

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.630
after handing him gifts from the emperor,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.700
he says something different,

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.499
but with the same general intent.

00:07:21.499 --> 00:07:24.440
Fa Zhou: The greatest gift and honor

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.503
is having you for a daughter.

00:07:27.503 --> 00:07:29.530
Narrator: When Mulan gets a
visit from Commander Tung,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:30.970
it's not a romantic gesture.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.710
Rather, he comes with a new sword,

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.040
as Mulan's was destroyed
during the final battle,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.780
and a second invitation from the emperor

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.030
to become an officer
in the emperor's guard.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.780
In the ballad, the animated movie,

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.100
and the live-action movie,

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.360
the emperor asks Mulan
to come work for him.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.080
Mulan always declines,

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.320
choosing instead to return home.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.430
The film ends before
she can give an answer.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.614
But it would make sense if
she wanted to fight again,

00:07:52.614 --> 00:07:54.516
because in the ballad,
Mulan and her comrades

00:07:54.516 --> 00:07:56.960
travel "together for twelve years."

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.890
Were there any other big
differences you spotted

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.070
between the animated and
the live-action films?

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:02.745
Let us know in the comments.

